diff --git a/docs/EXPERT_EVALUATION.md b/docs/EXPERT_EVALUATION.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e12e50d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/EXPERT_EVALUATION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# Expert evaluation protocol
+
+Automated metrics (citation recall, groundedness) do not tell us whether a workflow is *correct and usable*.
+This protocol has legal practitioners rate the system's output.
+
+## Procedure
+1. Run the benchmark: `python evals/eval_agent.py` (results JSON now stores each answer).
+2. Make blinded sheets: `python evals/expert/make_rating_sheet.py evals/results/eval_results_<ts>.json --experts 3 --sample 30`
+   — every expert gets the same cases in a different random order, with no system metrics visible.
+3. Each expert fills in their own `expert_N.csv` independently (no discussion before submitting).
+4. Aggregate: `python evals/expert/aggregate_expert.py evals/expert/sheets/expert_*.csv`
+
+Use at least 2 experts (3 preferred) with Indian corporate/tax/IP/employment/data-protection experience, and at least
+30 cases covering all five domains, multi-domain queries and abstentions.
+
+## Rubric (1 = very poor, 5 = excellent)
+| Column | Question |
+|---|---|
+| correctness_1to5 | Are the steps legally correct and in a sensible order for this query? |
+| legal_accuracy_1to5 | Does each cited provision actually support the step it is attached to? |
+| completeness_1to5 | Are important obligations for this situation missing? (5 = nothing material missing) |
+| usefulness_1to5 | Could a startup founder or counsel act on this without major rework? |
+| critical_error_yn | Y if any step is wrong in a way that could cause non-compliance or liability |
+| would_rely_yn | Y if you would use this as a starting point for client work |
+| comments | Free text; note missing statutes, outdated law (check the "as of" date), wrong domain |
+
+For **abstained** cases, rate whether abstaining was appropriate: correctness 5 = right call, 1 = the corpus did
+cover it. Usefulness for an abstention reflects how helpful the refusal message is.
+
+## Reported outputs
+Mean ± sd per dimension, % would-rely, % critical-error, usefulness over answered cases only, and quadratic-weighted
+kappa between each pair of experts. Report kappa alongside the means; a low kappa means the rubric needs
+calibration before the means can be trusted.
